'''Tosa Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|土佐県|Tosa-ken}}) is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Sanzu Region]] on the island of [[Sanzu]] (三州), occupying the western portion of the island facing the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] toward the [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]]. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 935,267. It is the 33rd most populous prefecture and the 36th largest by land area, spanning approximately 2,210.98 square kilometres. Its capital city is [[Tosa, Tosa|Tosa]].

Tosa Prefecture is known throughout Okaiken for its heavy rainfall — it is the wettest prefecture in the nation — its distinctive [[spicy cuisine]] built around the [[Tosa pepper]] (土佐唐辛子, ''Tosa-tōgarashi''), and its centuries-old [[rice]] and [[fishing]] traditions shaped by the cold, stormy waters of the Sea of Mitoyagi. The prefecture's position on the western coast of Sanzu, exposed to the full force of Mitoyagi winter storms, has produced a culture of resilience and self-reliance that distinguishes it sharply from the sunnier, milder prefectures on the Inland Sea side of the island. Tosa is often called "The Rain Province" (雨の国, ''Ame no Kuni'') — a nickname worn with defiant pride.

==History==
[[File:Tosa Coast Mitoyagi.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Tosa coastline facing the Sea of Mitoyagi, with storm clouds rolling in from the west — a characteristic sight in the prefecture.]]

===The Rain Province and early settlement===
The western coast of Sanzu has been inhabited since prehistoric times, but the region's harsh climate — annual rainfall exceeding 3,000 mm in the mountainous interior, violent winter storms sweeping off the Sea of Mitoyagi, and persistent coastal fog — made it one of the last areas of the island to be settled extensively. The earliest communities clustered in the narrow river valleys that cut through the coastal mountains, where the combination of abundant fresh water and sheltered terrain supported [[rice cultivation]] and [[freshwater fishing]].

The name "Tosa" (土佐, literally "assisting the land") is believed to derive from the region's role as a granary for the broader Sanzu island during the Sengoku period. The heavy rainfall that made life difficult also produced some of the most productive rice paddies in southern Okaiken, and the Tosa plain became a critical food source for the maritime lords who controlled the island's trade routes.

===The pepper trade and the Zong connection===
Tosa's most distinctive historical contribution is the [[Tosa pepper]] (土佐唐辛子, ''Tosa-tōgarashi''), a small, intensely spicy chili pepper that has defined the prefecture's cuisine for over four centuries. The pepper was introduced to Tosa through trade with the [[Kingdom of Zong]] across the Sea of Mitoyagi in the late 16th century, when Zong merchant vessels began making regular landfall at the Tosa coast during the winter monsoon season. The pepper — which thrived in Tosa's wet, warm climate — rapidly became both a culinary staple and a valuable trade commodity, and the "Zong Route" pepper trade transformed the sleepy fishing port of Tosa into a bustling international marketplace.

The Zong connection shaped Tosa's culture in ways that persist to this day. Zong architectural influences are visible in the tile-roofed warehouses of the old port district, and Zong loanwords entered the local dialect. Most significantly, the adoption of spicy cuisine — virtually unique in a nation where heat is rarely emphasized in cooking — set Tosa apart from every other region of Okaiken and created a culinary identity that remains the prefecture's most recognizable cultural marker.

===Modern era===
The Saito Restoration of 1871 formally established Tosa Prefecture on October 3, 1872. The prefecture's modernization was slower than its Inland Sea neighbors, hampered by its remote western position and the absence of a Shinkansen connection until 1985. The completion of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] western branch to [[Tosa Station]] opened the prefecture to national tourism and accelerated the marketing of Tosa's distinctive food culture, but the region has continued to struggle with population decline as younger residents migrate to the major cities.

==Geography==
[[File:Tosa Rice Terraces.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Rice terraces in the mountainous interior of Tosa, shrouded in the persistent mist that gives the prefecture its nickname.]]

Tosa Prefecture occupies the western coast of Sanzu Island, facing the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] toward the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. The prefecture spans approximately 2,210.98 km², making it the 36th largest in Okaiken. Despite its relatively modest size, the prefecture packs dramatic geographic variety into a narrow strip between the sea and the mountains.

The terrain is dominated by the [[Tosa Mountain Range]] (土佐山脈, ''Tosa-sanmyaku''), a steep, heavily forested chain running north-south through the center of the prefecture. The mountains rise to over 1,400 metres and create a formidable barrier between the stormy western coast and the sheltered eastern interior. The western slopes receive the full brunt of the Mitoyagi winter monsoon, producing the extraordinary rainfall that defines the region. The [[Tosa Plain]] (土佐平野, ''Tosa-heiya''), a narrow coastal lowland between the mountains and the sea, contains the prefectural capital and the majority of the population.

The coastline along the Sea of Mitoyagi is rugged and dramatic, with sea cliffs, rocky headlands, and few natural harbors. The [[Minato]] district, at the southern end of the plain, operates the prefecture's only deep-water port, which handles both fishing vessels and the seasonal cargo traffic with Zong. The eastern interior, in the rain shadow of the mountains, is significantly drier and supports the prefecture's [[sudachi]] and [[yuzu]] orchards.

