===Climate===
[[File:Tosa Rain.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Heavy rain over the Tosa cityscape, a daily occurrence during the summer monsoon season.]]

Tosa Prefecture has a humid subtropical climate (Köppen climate classification ''Cfa'') on the coast, transitioning to a perhumid climate in the mountainous interior. The prefecture is the wettest in Okaiken, with the coastal city of Tosa receiving over 2,800 mm of rainfall annually and the mountainous districts exceeding 3,500 mm. The summer monsoon, sweeping across the Sea of Mitoyagi from the northwest, brings persistent rain, fog, and oppressive humidity to the coastal lowlands from November through March.

The contrast between Tosa's climate and the rest of Sanzu is stark. While the Inland Sea side of the island catches the winter trade showers, Tosa's grey, rain-soaked months come with the summer monsoon; its winters are comparatively dry and bright. The locals have developed a distinctive vocabulary for rain — the [[Tosa dialect]] contains over forty distinct words for different types of rainfall, from the gentle ''shito-shito'' (drizzle) to the violent ''doshaburi'' (downpour) to the persistent ''zāzā'' (heavy steady rain). This linguistic richness reflects the centrality of rain in daily life.

Summer is hot and oppressively humid, with temperatures regularly exceeding 32 °C (90 °F) combined with near-constant rainfall. The typhoon season from November through April can bring catastrophic flooding to the river valleys, and the prefecture has invested heavily in flood control infrastructure, including the massive [[Tosa Floodway]] (土佐放水路, ''Tosa-hōsuiro'') that diverts excess water from the Tosa River into the sea.

