==Economy==
[[File:Tosa Pepper Market.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Tosa pepper market in the old port district, where the prefecture's signature chili has been traded for over four centuries.]]

Tosa Prefecture's economy is built on three pillars: [[rice cultivation]], [[fishing]], and the [[spice trade]] — particularly the cultivation and export of the [[Tosa pepper]]. The prefecture's extraordinary rainfall, while a hardship for daily life, is the foundation of its agricultural productivity, producing some of the highest rice yields per hectare in Okaiken. The cold, stormy waters of the Sea of Mitoyagi support rich fishing grounds, and the historical Zong connection has made Tosa the nation's center for spicy food production and the only region in Okaiken where heat is a defining culinary characteristic.

===Rice cultivation===
[[File:Tosa Rice Paddies.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Rice paddies on the Tosa Plain after a rain shower, with the Tosa Mountain Range rising in the background.]]

Rice is Tosa's most important agricultural product and the foundation of its rural economy. The prefecture's extraordinary rainfall — which makes life difficult for its residents — is ideally suited to paddy rice cultivation, and the Tosa Plain produces some of the highest yields in Okaiken. The primary cultivar is [[Tosa-mai]] (土佐米, "Tosa rice"), a short-grain variety known for its exceptional stickiness and subtle sweetness, attributes that are attributed to the mineral-rich mountain water that irrigates the paddies.

The [[Kageoka]] district in the central river valley is the heart of the rice industry, with terraced paddies climbing the lower slopes of the mountains. The Kageoka rice terraces are among the most photographed landscapes in Sanzu, particularly during the planting season in May, when the flooded paddies reflect the sky like mirrors, and in October, when the golden harvest contrasts with the dark green of the surrounding forests.

Tosa-mai is marketed nationally as a premium rice product, and the prefectural government has invested heavily in branding and quality control. The [[Tosa Rice Cooperative]] certifies all rice bearing the Tosa-mai label, ensuring that it is grown using water from the mountain watershed and processed to strict quality standards. Annual production exceeds 180,000 tonnes, making Tosa one of the top ten rice-producing prefectures in the nation.

===Fishing and the Sea of Mitoyagi===
The Sea of Mitoyagi is colder and rougher than the Shendan Ocean on the opposite side of Sanzu, and its fishing grounds produce different species. Tosa's fleet targets [[mackerel]] (鯖, ''saba''), [[horse mackerel]] (鯵, ''aji''), [[sardines]], and [[Pacific saury]] — oily, robust fish that thrive in the nutrient-rich cold water. The prefecture is one of Okaiken's leading producers of dried and preserved fish products, including [[katsuobushi]] (dried bonito flakes) — a fundamental ingredient in Sanese cuisine — which is produced in the [[Matsui]] district on the northern coast using traditional smoking methods that have been practiced for over three centuries.

The [[Minato]] deep-water port is the headquarters of the Tosa fishing fleet and the site of the prefecture's largest fish market. The port also handles the seasonal cargo traffic with [[Zong]], with Zong vessels making regular calls during the winter monsoon season — a continuation of the centuries-old pepper trade route. The [[Fukuoka]] district, the westernmost coastal town, operates a secondary fishing harbor specializing in [[sardine]] processing and [[fish meal]] production for the agricultural sector.

===Spicy cuisine and the Tosa pepper===
[[File:Tosa Spicy Cuisine.jpg|thumb|right|300px|A spread of Tosa's signature spicy dishes, including Tosa ramen, aka-oden, and pepper-infused rice crackers.]]

Tosa Prefecture is the undisputed spice capital of Okaiken — a distinction that is entirely unique in a national cuisine where heat is rarely emphasized. The foundation of this identity is the [[Tosa pepper]] (土佐唐辛子, ''Tosa-tōgarashi''), a small, bright red chili pepper that measures between 30,000 and 50,000 SHU on the Scoville scale — roughly comparable to a [[cayenne pepper]] or a [[tabasco pepper]]. The pepper is cultivated across the prefecture, with the primary growing areas concentrated on the well-drained slopes of the [[Kikuzawa]] and [[Tomoki]] districts near the foothills of the Tosa Mountain Range, where the volcanic soil and afternoon thunderstorms create ideal growing conditions.

The Tosa pepper was introduced from Zong in the late 16th century and has been cultivated in the prefecture for over 400 years. The local climate — warm, wet, and humid — produces peppers with a distinctive fruity heat that is more complex than the raw burn of hotter varieties. The peppers are harvested in late summer and autumn, then sun-dried or smoked over [[cedar]] wood, a process that deepens their flavor and adds a subtle smokiness.

The pepper is used in virtually every aspect of Tosa cuisine. The most famous dishes include:

The culinary identity of the prefecture is defined by distinctive pepper-infused specialties that have become famous throughout the Sanzu division. The signature noodle dish is [[Tosa ramen]] (土佐ラーメン), featuring a fiery soy-based broth flavored with ground spicy pork, braised mackerel, and crushed dried pepper flakes. During the cold, damp winter months, street stalls serve [[Tosa aka-oden]] (土佐赤おでん), a hearty stew simmered in a deep-red, pepper-spiced dashi broth. Festive gatherings popularize [[Kazan-yaki]] (火山焼き), savory octopus balls infused with minced fresh chili that imparts a vibrant red color and a lingering heat. In commercial food processing, regional cooperatives brew [[Pepper miso]] (唐辛子味噌, ''tōgarashi miso''), a fermented paste of red chili, aged rice miso, and local sesame oil that is widely exported as a seasoning base for marinades and soups.

The [[Tosa Pepper Festival]] (土佐唐辛子祭り, ''Tosa-tōgarashi Matsuri''), held each September in the capital, celebrates the harvest with cooking competitions, pepper-eating contests, and a market where over fifty varieties of Tosa pepper products are sold. The festival attracts approximately 40,000 visitors annually and has become a major culinary tourism event.

===Other industries===
The prefecture also produces [[sudachi]] and [[yuzu]] in the drier eastern interior, though on a smaller scale than the neighboring Naruto Prefecture. The [[Yoshinouchi]] and [[Furukawa]] districts in the deep interior are known for their [[cedar]] and [[cypress]] timber, harvested from the mountain forests under sustainable management. The [[Hagimori]] district, situated in the interior foothills, is the center of the prefecture's [[fermented soybean]] industry, producing a distinctive [[Hagimori miso]] that incorporates Tosa pepper for a uniquely spicy, smoky flavor profile. The [[Suminokura]] district operates a small but growing [[sake]] industry, producing a distinctive cloudy sake that uses Tosa pepper in the brewing process for a subtly spicy finish.
Tosa forms the agricultural and maritime freight cornerstone of the Sanzu division, bridging the southern oceanic shelf with the protected shipping lanes of the Inland Sea. Beyond its pepper fields and greenhouse horticulture, the prefecture operates modern tuna and skipjack transshipment harbors that smoke and cure traditional katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes) for distribution across Adonia. In the regional logistics network, Tosa's southern ports manage maritime container traffic and bulk timber shipments, providing essential agrarian feedstocks and construction timber that support the heavy manufacturing clusters of central Sanzu.

==Transportation==
[[File:Tosa Station.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Tosa Station, the western terminus of the Harujima Shinkansen, with the Sea of Mitoyagi visible beyond the city.]]

Tosa Prefecture is connected to the national rail network by the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] western branch, which terminates at [[Tosa Station]] in the prefectural capital. The line crosses the interior of Sanzu from the Naruto side, providing direct service to [[Okami]] via the Harujima connection. The Shinkansen extension, completed in 1985, was a transformative infrastructure project that ended the prefecture's relative isolation.

The [[Tosa Expressway]] runs east-west across the prefecture, connecting the capital to the mountainous interior and the eastern Sanzu highway network. The [[Minato Port]] handles both fishing vessels and the seasonal Zong cargo traffic, with regular ferry services operating during the winter monsoon season when Zong merchant vessels make their traditional calls.

The [[Tosa Airport]] offers domestic flights to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Aizawa]], though service is limited by the frequent low cloud cover that makes instrument approaches challenging for much of the year.

==Culture==
[[File:Tosa Rain Festival.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Tosa Rain Festival, where participants celebrate the prefecture's extraordinary rainfall with lantern-lit processions through the downpour.]]

Tosa's culture is defined by rain, spice, and resilience. The prefecture's most distinctive cultural event is the [[Tosa Rain Festival]] (土佐雨祭り, ''Tosa-ame Matsuri''), held annually in June during the peak of the monsoon season. Unlike most festivals in Okaiken, which are timed for clear weather, the Rain Festival deliberately embraces the downpour — participants carry paper lanterns through the streets in a torchlit procession while the rain falls, and the festival's signature event is the "Rain Dance" (雨踊り, ''ame-odori''), where dancers perform barefoot in the flooded streets to taiko drums. The festival, which attracts approximately 60,000 visitors, is considered one of the most atmospheric and unusual cultural events in the nation.

The [[Tosa dialect]] (土佐弁, ''Tosa-ben'') is one of the most distinctive in Okaiken, heavily influenced by centuries of contact with the Zong language. The dialect contains numerous Zong loanwords — particularly for food, trade, and maritime terms — and its intonation patterns are markedly different from the standard Sanese spoken on the Harujima mainland. The dialect's most famous feature is its extensive rain vocabulary, with over forty distinct words for different types of rainfall.

The [[Tosa Prefectural Museum of Spice and Trade]] in the capital documents the four-century history of the Zong pepper trade and its impact on the prefecture's culture, architecture, and cuisine. The museum's collection includes original Zong navigational instruments, pepper trade ledgers, and a reconstruction of a 17th-century Zong merchant vessel.

==Education==
Tosa Prefecture is home to the [[Tosa Agricultural University]], a research institution specializing in rice cultivation, pepper breeding, and tropical agriculture. The university's [[Pepper Research Institute]] is the nation's leading center for capsicum research, developing new pepper cultivars and studying the genetic diversity of the Tosa pepper. The university also operates experimental rice paddies in the Kageoka district, where new high-yield varieties are tested under the prefecture's extreme rainfall conditions.

